<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral conjunctivitis (B30.9)</content>. Bilateral follicular conjunctivitis with watery discharge, a preauricular node, and a sore throat after contact with an affected student: the pattern of adenoviral conjunctivitis. Vision is normal, the cornea is clear, and there is no pain or photophobia, so there is no sign of keratitis, iritis, or acute glaucoma. The AAO advises against antibiotic drops for viral conjunctivitis, and topical corticosteroids are not started without an eye examination by an ophthalmologist. [S]<list><item>No antibiotic or steroid drops; cool compresses and preservative-free artificial tears.</item><item>Hand washing, no eye rubbing, his own towel and pillowcase; students to clean shared brushes and tools; it is contagious for up to two weeks.</item><item>Same-day return for eye pain, light sensitivity, reduced vision, or symptoms beyond two weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Ulnar neuropathy at the right elbow (G56.21)</content>. Unchanged; wearing the splints. [S]<list><item>Continue splinting and hand therapy as planned.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Conjunctivitis Preferred Practice Pattern (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Annual wellness visit next week as booked; same day for the warning signs above. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-01: subsequent annual wellness visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
